          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.3 Effect of Air and Water on Other Metals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.166,
            0.683,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 4.6: Let us investigate (demonstration activity)**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.204,
            0.533,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": "The teacher may demonstrate this activity."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.22,
            0.115,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.225,
            0.743,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "**Caution** — It is advisable for students to wear protective eyeglasses"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.243,
            0.328,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "and keep safe distance."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.262,
            0.474,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": "Take a magnesium ribbon about 3 – 4 centimetres long. Clean it by rubbing with a piece of sandpaper."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.341,
            0.472,
            0.398
          ],
          "content": "Hold it with a pair of tongs. Ignite the other end using a spirit lamp or a candle (Fig. 4.5)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.402,
            0.471,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "Let the magnesium ribbon burn."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.426,
            0.379,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": "What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.45,
            0.471,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "You must have observed that"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.488,
            0.247,
            0.893,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.529,
                0.441,
                0.852,
                0.46
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.5: Burning magnesium ribbon"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.468,
            0.744,
            0.544
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "magnesium ribbon burns with a dazzling white flame and changes into a white powder. Collect it on a watch glass. This powder is magnesium oxide. It is formed due to the reaction between magnesium and oxygen present in the air."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.548,
            0.743,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "Add a few drops of warm water to this white powder, stir it well, and check its nature."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.59,
            0.744,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "Recall the chapter ‘Exploring Substances: Acidic, Basic, and Neutral’. Find out whether the solution of magnesium oxide is acidic or basic or neutral in nature. You can use any acid-base indicator."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.669,
            0.51,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "What effect does this solution have on blue and red litmus papers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.712,
            0.511,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "You would observe that it changes the colour of red litmus paper to blue (Fig. 4.6); hence, it is basic in nature. Generally, oxides of metals are basic in nature."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.806,
            0.512,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": "We will discuss this burning of magnesium ribbon further in the chapter ‘Changes Around Us: Physical and Chemical’."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.526,
            0.658,
            0.904,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.56,
                0.845,
                0.798,
                0.879
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.6: Testing the nature of magnesium oxide"
            }
          ],
